# The DAG-CBOR block a DefCID addresses, as an IPLD Schema.
#
# Root ADR adr-2609076000-kir-is-an-ipld-codec-schema-and-adl, decision 2:
# the KIR block shape is an explicit IPLD Schema, written in ipld.schema-dsl
# and validated as Schema DMT by ipld.schema/compile-schema -- structure,
# references, representation tables and named-reference closure pinned here
# rather than in prose.
#
# What is described is the output of
# `kotoba.kir.definition-identity/normalize` over `identity-payload`, which
# is what `cbor/encode` is handed and therefore what the CID addresses. The
# docstring on `canonical-bytes` is the authority for that: "These bytes --
# not any printed representation of them -- are the thing the CID addresses."
#
# TWO THINGS THIS SCHEMA CANNOT PIN, named here rather than left to be
# discovered. Both are properties of IPLD Schema, measured 2026-09-10 against
# io-ipld 973e26a5, not oversights:
#
#   1. THE POSITIONAL DISCRIMINATOR. A node is a list whose element 0 is the
#      tag: ["int" "5"]. IPLD Schema's union strategies are keyed, kinded,
#      envelope, inline, stringprefix and bytesprefix -- every one of them
#      discriminates on a map key, a data-model kind, or a string/byte
#      prefix. None reads a discriminant out of list position 0, and a kinded
#      union over these variants is refused at compile time as an ambiguous
#      table because all twelve are the `list` kind. So `Node` below is
#      declared as a bare list and the DISPATCH is the checker's, not the
#      schema's.
#
#   2. THE TAG BOUND TO ITS VARIANT. `tag Tag` admits all twelve members in
#      every variant, so ["nil" "5"] unifies against IntNode. Pinning one
#      member per struct would need a singleton type; `unit` does not carry a
#      string value. The checker asserts the tag it dispatched on equals the
#      tag the node carries.
#
# What IS pinned here, and is therefore no longer prose: the closed tag
# vocabulary, the arity of every variant (one element for nil, two for the
# rest), the data-model kind of every body, the entry shape inside a map, and
# the named-reference closure that ties the four collection bodies back to
# Node.
#
# NOT PINNED ON PURPOSE -- decision 4: the schema pins SHAPE, not
# CHECKEDNESS. Native typed-feature admission, the closed scalar-variant
# export boundary, typing, effect rows and the lowering budget are not
# expressible here and are not attempted. A block that unifies against this
# schema and then fails admission must not report what one that passes both
# reports.
#
# NOT SEALED INTO THE IDENTITY -- decision 5: this schema's version and the
# representation it describes MUST NOT enter the identity payload. Sealing
# them would move every definition's identity on a purely representational
# change. The six sealed inputs named by lang/code-identity.edn are
# unchanged, and adding this file changed no byte of any block.

# --- the closed tag vocabulary -------------------------------------------
# Every branch of `normalize`, and nothing else. `normalize` throws for a
# value outside this domain rather than coercing it, so the enum is closed on
# the producing side as well as here.

type Tag enum {
  | Nil   ("nil")
  | Bool  ("bool")
  | Int   ("int")
  | Str   ("str")
  | Kw    ("kw")
  | Sym   ("sym")
  | Bytes ("bytes")
  | F64   ("f64")
  | Map   ("map")
  | Set   ("set")
  | Vec   ("vec")
  | List  ("list")
} representation string

# --- a node ---------------------------------------------------------------
# See limitation 1: this is the widest type IPLD Schema can give the union.
# The checker narrows it by reading element 0 and unifying against the
# variant below that the tag names.

type Node [Any]

# --- scalar variants ------------------------------------------------------
# Integers, floats and bytes are carried as TEXT, not as CBOR numbers. A
# 64-bit KIR literal exceeds JavaScript's exact-integer range and an f64 bit
# pattern routinely does too, so text is what keeps :clj and :cljs on the
# same bytes. That is why `value String` and not `value Int` below, and it is
# a property of the identity, not of this file.

type NilNode   struct { tag Tag                } representation tuple
type BoolNode  struct { tag Tag  value Bool    } representation tuple
type IntNode   struct { tag Tag  value String  } representation tuple
type StrNode   struct { tag Tag  value String  } representation tuple
type KwNode    struct { tag Tag  value String  } representation tuple
type SymNode   struct { tag Tag  value String  } representation tuple
type BytesNode struct { tag Tag  value String  } representation tuple
type F64Node   struct { tag Tag  value String  } representation tuple

# --- collection variants --------------------------------------------------
# Ordering is canonical and total (`cmp`), so a set and a map have one
# sequence on every implementation. Order is not expressible here; it is the
# producer's invariant and the differential test's business.

type VecNode  struct { tag Tag  value [Node]  } representation tuple
type ListNode struct { tag Tag  value [Node]  } representation tuple
type SetNode  struct { tag Tag  value [Node]  } representation tuple
type MapNode  struct { tag Tag  value [Entry] } representation tuple

type Entry struct { key Node  value Node } representation tuple
